# Figure it out

No playbook matches the task? Design one. Deliverable before code: the workflow itself. Phases that scale rigor to the task, run the scientific method, leave a decision trail a human can audit after stepping away.

## Start

Open a todolist. First item: read the Actions section of the [saber mode](../saber-mode/SKILL.md) skill. Then add the phases below as todos.

## Phase A: Frame

Ground first, then commit. Don't start until you can state:

- Definition of done as a falsifiable predicate (the [prove it works](../prove-it-works/SKILL.md) principle skill).
- Scope, quantified: rough units and effort, plus blockers grounding surfaced.
- Rigor level, biased high. One-way doors and high blast radius get more. Reversible low-stakes steps get less. Rigor = gates and artifacts, not "try harder".

Present framing and tradeoffs before committing to a long run. Reversible work proceeds (the [never block](../never-block/SKILL.md) principle skill). A multi-hour run earns one checkpoint.

## Phase B: Design the workflow

Decompose into atomic, independently-landable units. Riskiest-unknown first. Scaffold and verification before features (the [foundational thinking](../foundational-thinking/SKILL.md) principle skill).

- Build the verification harness before the work. Capture the baseline from the pre-change state, so the check reads "old value vs new value".
- One-way-door design decisions: run the [architect](../architect/SKILL.md) skill (it runs [arena](../arena/SKILL.md)). Skip for mechanical work with a concrete shape. A second arena over a settled design is over-engineering (the [laziness protocol](../laziness-protocol/SKILL.md) principle skill).
- Decide what fans out. Parallelize only across seams. Give each worker its own worktree or branch (the [separate before serializing shared state](../separate-before-serializing-shared-state/SKILL.md) principle skill). Don't over-fan.
- Write the phase list down. That list is what the human reviews.

Then execute. Add its steps to the todolist as concrete items, after the Phase C entry and before Phase D. Run each under the Phase C loop discipline. Weave the Phase D log through them, a row as each step lands, not one trail at the end.

## Phase C: Run the loop

Each unit is an experiment. State the hypothesis, make the smallest change, measure against the predicate on the real artifact, keep it if it advanced, revert if not.
Apply the [sequence verifiable units](../sequence-verifiable-units/SKILL.md) principle skill, verifying each unit before the next instead of batching checks at the end.

- Verify by inspecting the artifact, never a self-report. Passes too easily: suspect the observation method before the system.
- Pair delegated work with a judge. Worker games the gate: reset and harden the contract. Gate itself wrong: fix the gate in its own change, don't route around it.
- Verdict: VERIFIED, NOT VERIFIED, or INCONCLUSIVE. Inconclusive is not a pass. Don't hide a negative.

## Phase D: Keep the audit trail

Log the run via the [show me your work](../show-me-your-work/SKILL.md) skill. figure-it-out work is usually ambitious enough to commit the trail so the reviewer reads it in the PR. Trail plus diff lets the human come back and trust the work.

## Phase E: Verify and hand back

Check the whole against the Phase A predicate on the real product, not just the harness. Encode any recurring correction as a gate, lint rule, check, or script (the [encode lessons in structure](../encode-lessons-in-structure/SKILL.md) principle skill).

**Reply:** the playbook you designed, the rigor level and why, the decision-trail path, what's verified against the predicate, and what's still open.
